<model>
    <mount>//OPNsense/FanCtl</mount>
    <version>1.0.0</version>
    <description>Fan control</description>
    <items>
        <!--
            Everything lives in this one container, and the names are not free.
            Nothing here is read through the model at run time: the controller is Python,
            started by an rc.d service, and fanctl.py reads ./OPNsense/FanCtl/general out of
            /conf/config.xml and looks up every one of these by name. Rename one, or move it
            into a container of its own, and the settings page saves a value that nothing
            ever reads - which for most plugins means a setting that does nothing, and for
            this one means a floor that is not applied.

            Every bound below is also enforced in settings() in fanctl.py, because the daemon
            reads config.xml with no model in front of it: a file restored from a backup or
            edited by hand arrives there unchecked. Where the two ever disagree the narrower
            wins, and the narrower is the Python one, because that is the copy with a fan
            behind it.
        -->
        <general>
            <enabled type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <!--
                    Off. A plugin that starts writing to fan registers on the strength of
                    having been installed is a plugin that can stop the only fan in somebody
                    else's chassis, on a machine whose headers it has never seen. No amount of
                    probing beforehand makes that decision safe to take on somebody's behalf.
                -->
                <Default>0</Default>
            </enabled>
            <!--
                What the daemon is allowed to do. Three values and only three, because the
                interesting line is between the first and the other two: monitor writes
                nothing at all, ever, and is what makes this plugin installable on an
                appliance nobody has measured - the page fills with real readings and the chip
                is never touched.
            -->
            <mode type="OptionField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>monitor</Default>
                <OptionValues>
                    <monitor>Watch only - never write to the chip</monitor>
                    <curve>Follow the temperature curve</curve>
                    <manual>Hold one fixed duty</manual>
                </OptionValues>
            </mode>
            <!--
                Which of its own two curves a board that drives its fans itself runs (2026-10-30).
                Only the AMDA0202 board has two today - the XGS 2100, 2300, 3100 and 3300, whose
                CPLD drives both fans on the CPU's temperature and has no duty register - and the
                table says so in its board_curve block; on any other machine this does nothing.
                Standard is whatever the board's own software chose, which is not touched; quiet
                drives the coprocessor's GPIO 48 low, as the vendor does on the 2100 and 2300. It
                is applied only while the plugin is enabled. fanctl.py reads it without this
                model (settings()) and refuses anything but these two.
            -->
            <board_curve type="OptionField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>standard</Default>
                <OptionValues>
                    <standard>Standard - the curve the board chose itself</standard>
                    <quiet>Quiet - the lower curve of the same board</quiet>
                </OptionValues>
            </board_curve>
            <!--
                Which channels the daemon may drive, by the name the chip gives them. Empty
                is the default and empty means none: a list that defaulted to "everything the
                probe found" would drive a header with nothing on it the first time somebody
                switched the mode over, and on this chip four of the five channels report no
                fan at all.

                A plain list rather than a set of checkboxes, because the names come from the
                hardware and differ between chips. The page offers what the probe actually
                found; the mask here only holds each entry to the shape of a channel name.
            -->
            <channels type="CSVListField">
                <MaskPerItem>Y</MaskPerItem>
                <Mask>/^[A-Z][A-Z0-9_]{1,15}$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>Use channel names as the chip reports them, such as SYSFAN.</ValidationMessage>
            </channels>
            <!--
                Which temperatures a decision is made from. The hottest reading among the
                groups chosen is what the curve is fed - never an average, because one core at
                85 C and three at 40 C averages to a number a curve is happy with while the
                machine still has a core at 85 C.

                A group that is chosen and not present on the machine is not ignored: it fails
                the sensors line of the safety contract and the fans go back to the hardware,
                because a decision made from a sensor that is not answering is a guess.

                The mask is the whole of GROUPS in sensors.py and has to stay that way. The
                discovery finds drives, board inputs, network chips and memory modules on
                machines that have them; a mask narrower than what can be discovered is a
                sensor the page can see in the list and refuse to save.
            -->
            <sensors type="CSVListField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>cpu,pch</Default>
                <MaskPerItem>Y</MaskPerItem>
                <Mask>/^(cpu|pch|acpi|nvme|drive|board|nic|dimm|other)$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>Choose from the sensor groups this machine reports.</ValidationMessage>
            </sensors>
            <!--
                How often a decision is made. Two seconds is the floor because the tachometer
                on this class of chip is itself averaged over a window and a loop faster than
                the sensor tells you nothing new; 120 is the ceiling because the watchdog is
                six cycles long and a ten-minute watchdog is not one.
            -->
            <interval type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>10</Default>
                <MinimumValue>2</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>120</MaximumValue>
                <ValidationMessage>A decision is made every 2 to 120 seconds.</ValidationMessage>
            </interval>

            <!--
                The floor, and the most important number on this page.

                It is NOT the floor the plugin drives to. The floor it drives to is the higher
                of this and the one measured on the hardware with the floor test, and where
                nothing has been measured the plugin drives nothing at all whatever is typed
                here. This is the owner's own limit on top of that - "I do not want this fan
                below 40% whatever the curve says" - and it can only ever raise the floor,
                never lower it. 20 is the lowest it accepts, not because 20 is safe on any
                particular fan, but because a page that let somebody type 0 would be a page
                that looked as though 0 were a setting.
            -->
            <floor_duty type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>40</Default>
                <MinimumValue>20</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>100</MaximumValue>
                <ValidationMessage>The floor is a percentage between 20 and 100.</ValidationMessage>
            </floor_duty>
            <!-- what the manual mode holds. Held up to the floor, never down to it. -->
            <manual_duty type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>70</Default>
                <MinimumValue>20</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>100</MaximumValue>
                <ValidationMessage>A duty is a percentage between 20 and 100.</ValidationMessage>
            </manual_duty>
            <!--
                The curve: temperature:percent, in rising order of temperature. Stored as one
                string rather than as a grid of its own because it is read back out of
                config.xml by a Python daemon with no model to help it, and because four
                numbers are easier to read in one line than in four rows. curve.py parses it
                again at that end and refuses what it cannot read, which is the second of the
                two checks - this mask is the first.
            -->
            <points type="TextField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>40:60,50:75,60:90,70:100</Default>
                <Mask>/^\s*\d{1,3}\s*:\s*\d{1,3}\s*(,\s*\d{1,3}\s*:\s*\d{1,3}\s*){1,7}$/</Mask>
                <ValidationMessage>Write the curve as degrees:percent pairs, such as 40:60,50:75,60:90,70:100 - between two and eight of them.</ValidationMessage>
            </points>
            <!--
                Above this temperature the curve is not consulted at all and the duty is 100,
                applied at once, whatever the dwell and the hysteresis would otherwise have
                said.
            -->
            <limit_c type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>70</Default>
                <MinimumValue>40</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>100</MaximumValue>
                <ValidationMessage>The limit is a temperature between 40 and 100 C.</ValidationMessage>
            </limit_c>
            <!--
                And above THIS one the plugin stops being in the way: it hands every fan back
                to the hardware and the daemon exits. A machine that is still climbing at full
                duty has demonstrated that this plugin is not what is keeping it cool, and the
                most useful thing it can then do is return the chip to a control law that was
                written by whoever built the board.
            -->
            <panic_c type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>85</Default>
                <MinimumValue>45</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>110</MaximumValue>
                <ValidationMessage>The panic point is a temperature between 45 and 110 C.</ValidationMessage>
            </panic_c>

            <!--
                The two numbers that stop a fan hunting, and they only ever apply on the way
                down. Going up is immediate: a dwell on the way up would be a dwell spent hot.
            -->
            <hysteresis_c type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>4</Default>
                <MinimumValue>0</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>20</MaximumValue>
                <ValidationMessage>The hysteresis is 0 to 20 C.</ValidationMessage>
            </hysteresis_c>
            <dwell_seconds type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>30</Default>
                <MinimumValue>0</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>600</MaximumValue>
                <ValidationMessage>The dwell is 0 to 600 seconds.</ValidationMessage>
            </dwell_seconds>
            <!--
                The duty is rounded UP to this. Most of what a temperature does in a firewall
                is wobble by a degree, and a duty that followed every degree would be a write
                to the chip every interval for ever - which matters here more than it would
                elsewhere, because the kernel's own reader of this chip takes no lock and
                every write of ours is a window against it. Up rather than to nearest, so that
                the fan is never slower than the curve asked for.
            -->
            <step type="IntegerField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>5</Default>
                <MinimumValue>1</MinimumValue>
                <MaximumValue>25</MaximumValue>
                <ValidationMessage>The step is 1 to 25 percent.</ValidationMessage>
            </step>

            <!--
                The one field on this page that is a signature rather than a setting.

                The shipped table refuses control on any model nobody has measured, which is
                every model but the one the plugin was written on - and on that one too, until
                its floor has been measured. This is how somebody says "this is my machine, I
                have read what the page says about it, I am watching it". It does not lower the
                floor, it does not skip the floor measurement, and it does not silence
                anything. It does exactly two things: it satisfies the permission line of the
                safety contract, leaving the other five to hold on their own, and it allows the
                floor test to run on hardware whose chip the table does not describe - which the
                test otherwise refuses, and without which no unlisted appliance could ever gain
                its first measurement and this table could never grow.
            -->
            <accept_unmeasured type="BooleanField">
                <Required>Y</Required>
                <Default>0</Default>
            </accept_unmeasured>
        </general>
    </items>
</model>
